hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat(server): drive the image decode budget AND render worker count from a
    single cgroup-aware memory budget, replacing the hardcoded 16 MP decode cap
    and the raw-CPU-count worker heuristic. New resource_limits.py reads the real
    cgroup memory/CPU limits (docker --memory / systemd MemoryMax / k8s), which
    psutil and os.cpu_count() miss — that blind spot let the server size itself to
    the host and OOM inside a constrained container. resource_budget.py derives
    both numbers from the budget, calibrated to reproduce the proven 464 MB Pi
    operating point (~16 MP, 1 worker) so the appliance does not regress, while a
    1 GB+ box now renders a 38.9 MP panorama the old cap wrongly rejected.
  * feat(config): new memory_budget_mb setting (0 = auto/cgroup-aware, N = cap
    clamped to physical RAM) with a GUI control showing detected memory and the
    derived decode budget / workers. Removes the image_worker_thread_count knob
    (config + GUI) — the worker count is now always derived. Config schema v9.
  * fix(server): reject a memory_budget_mb below the usable floor (~366 MB) with a
    clear error instead of silently producing a zero decode budget (which made
    Clear-caches churn and OOM the Pi); a stale too-low value is coerced to auto.
  * ux: report image-size limits in megapixels (matching the config/UI) in the
    failed-conversion and upload-rejection messages, not raw pixels.
  * test: add a deb-in-docker end-to-end smoke harness (build .deb -> install ->
    boot -> upload every test image -> validate dither) under test_server/e2e/.

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 14:55:29 -0500

hokku-server (4.0.0~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First public beta of the 4.0 Pi Zero 2 W appliance line. Highlights since
    the 4.0 alphas: bounded, OOM-safe image decode on the 464 MB board (ingest
    decode budget rejects un-draftable sources over 16 MP before any phase
    decodes them; shrink-first + reduce-staged downscale; JPEG draft), so a
    mixed library — big JPEGs, HEIF, a 38.9 MP HEIF panorama — converts without
    crash-looping. Serve on waitress (bounded thread pool) instead of the
    Werkzeug dev server. Dropped the RLIMIT_AS render guard that broke native
    decoders (JPEG XL). First-boot rootfs resize, pre-warmed numba dither cache,
    pinned numeric-stack threads, USB gadget serial console, and the captive-
    portal setup wizard. Validated end-to-end on hardware: flash -> boot ->
    wizard -> WiFi -> serve, and the full reference set converts (15 ok, the one
    oversized panorama refused gracefully).

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 03:16:33 +0000

hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.25-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(render): drop the RLIMIT_AS memory guard from the render worker — it broke
    native decoders. JPEG XL failed with an opaque libjxl "Generic Error" (only in
    the worker, only on the Pi): reproduced as libjxl reserving large *virtual*
    arenas/thread-stacks beyond the RLIMIT_AS cap. That cap was virtual-only, so it
    never prevented the *physical* OOM it was meant to (that is now handled at
    ingest by DECODE_BUDGET_PIXELS, which refuses an oversized source before any
    phase decodes it) — redundant and harmful, so removed. Adds a JXL render
    smoke-test so a native-decoder regression can't return silently. Confirmed on
    hardware: the .jxl reference image now renders.

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sat, 25 Jul 2026 22:31:28 +0000

hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.24-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(server): serve on waitress with a bounded request-thread pool instead of
    the Werkzeug dev server. The dev server spawned one thread per HTTP request
    and hit the Pi's process limit ("RuntimeError: can't start new thread") when
    several screens polled static assets/thumbnails at once. waitress uses a
    fixed pool (AppConfig.server_threads, default 4) — plenty for this appliance,
    where request handlers just return cached bytes and rendering runs in its own
    worker. Also drops the "development server" warning. New python3-waitress
    dependency. (The JXL "Generic Error" seen during the earlier OOM was libjxl
    failing to allocate under memory pressure; it decodes fine now that the
    ingest gate keeps memory in budget — no code change needed there.)

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sat, 25 Jul 2026 22:16:55 +0000

hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.23-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(server): gate over-budget images at INGEST, not just at render. dev.22
    only guarded the render decode; the thumbnail (Phase 1) and classify (Phase 2)
    phases decode at full resolution too, so a 38.9 MP HEIF still OOM-killed the
    Pi before render ran. The dimension-reading choke point (_try_read_image_dims)
    now refuses an un-draftable source over DECODE_BUDGET_PIXELS the same way it
    refuses an unreadable one — the record is marked "failed" and drops out of the
    thumbnail/classify/render phases entirely. Shared, dims-only
    decoded_pixels_exceed_budget() mirrors the render-path accept/reject (JPEG
    passes on its post-draft size). Verified live on hardware: the full reference
    set (incl. 32 MP + 26 MP JPEGs and a 12 MP HEIF) converts with no OOM.

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sat, 25 Jul 2026 21:14:32 +0000

hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.22-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(server): stop large images OOM-killing the Pi appliance. Decode now
    shrinks BEFORE EXIF-transpose / RGB-convert / white-composite (previously
    all ran at full source resolution, holding 3+ full-frame buffers at once),
    and a two-stage reduce()+LANCZOS downscale keeps the resize temp small.
    A post-draft DECODE_BUDGET_PIXELS (16 MP) refuses un-draftable sources that
    would still blow the RAM budget (e.g. a 38.9 MP HEIF panorama) with a clear
    "downscale it" message instead of crash-looping — big JPEGs pass via draft
    shrink-on-load. Source bbox lowered 2x→1.5x screen. Adds decode-path unit
    tests (orientation, transparency, budget accept/reject, peak guards) and a
    CI-runnable shrink-first invariant, since the peak-memory guards were all
    time_intensive and never ran in CI.

 -- Dennis Fleurbaaij <mail@dennisfleurbaaij.com>  Sat, 25 Jul 2026 19:31:57 +0000

hokku-server (4.0.1~dev.21-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(server): pin the numeric stack's threads (OPENBLAS/OMP/MKL=1, numba=2).
    Un-pinned, numpy/scipy/OpenBLAS grabbed 4 threads/core and blew past the
    Pi's RLIMIT_NPROC + thrashed RAM — a cold-cache re-JIT boot never settled.

